## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical webhook security testing skills at a beginner level, focusing on cryptographic verification and attack scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- HMAC verification
- Timing-safe comparisons
- Timestamp tolerance
- Replay protection
- Test harness usage

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js example
const crypto = require('crypto');
function sign(body, secret){
  return crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret).update(body).digest('hex');
}
function verify(body, ts, provided, secret){
  const expected = sign(body, secret);
  return crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(provided,'hex'), Buffer.from(expected,'hex')) && Math.abs(Date.now() - Number(ts)) < 300000;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate replay protection in a distributed environment?
- What changes if the webhook payload is large or binary?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the candidate's ability to design practical test strategies for complex, cache-driven multi-tenant systems. It tests understanding of data isolation, concurrency handling, and resilience patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant data isolation in Redis cache
- Pagination semantics under high concurrency
- CSV export validity, encoding, and integrity
- Cache eviction patterns and Redis outage handling
- Background worker refresh logic and data consistency

## Code Example
